lesson.body:
Before a debugger can show you a variable's value, and before a print statement can report it, you have to know what value to expect in the first place. Code tracing is how you get there: it means manually stepping through code, on paper or in your head, tracking every variable's value and which branches or loops execute, exactly the way the computer would, one line at a time.

This depends directly on control flow, the if statements, loops, and function calls that determine which lines actually run and in what order. Tracing means following those same control flow decisions yourself instead of letting the interpreter make them silently. It is also the mental skill underneath every debugging tool covered so far: print debugging shows you a value, a debugger pauses on a value, but tracing is what lets you predict what that value should be, so you can recognize when it is wrong.

A good way to trace code is to draw a small table, with one column per variable and one row per line or loop iteration, updating it as you mentally execute each step. Consider this function, meant to return the first even number in a list:

def first_even(numbers):
    for i in range(len(numbers) - 1):
        if numbers[i] % 2 == 0:
            return numbers[i]
    return None

Call first_even([3, 5, 7, 8]). The only even number in the list is 8, at index 3, so the function should return 8. Trace it by hand. len(numbers) is 4, so range(len(numbers) - 1) is range(3), producing the values 0, 1, 2, not 3. On i = 0, numbers[0] is 3, and 3 % 2 equals 1, not 0, so the if is false. On i = 1, numbers[1] is 5, again odd. On i = 2, numbers[2] is 7, again odd. The loop then ends, because range(3) never produces 3, and the function falls through to return None.

The trace makes the bug obvious in a way that reading the code casually might not: the loop never checks index 3 at all, because range(len(numbers) - 1) stops one short of the last valid index. The fix is to use range(len(numbers)) instead. Notice that nothing here required running the program; the bug was found entirely by tracking the value of i and numbers[i] by hand across each pass through the loop, and comparing what the code actually does against what it was supposed to do. That is the core skill behind debugging, useful even when you are reading someone else's code for a review, or reasoning about a design before you have written any code to run at all.
